# ACCEPT -- weasyprint: url_fetcher bypass in write_pdf(xmp_metadata=...) and
# write_pdf/render(stylesheets=...) — two call sites build a fresh default
# URLFetcher instead of using the document's restrictive one, allowing
# file:// reads / SSRF when either param carries an attacker-influenced
# URL. Morse's only usage (apps/api/apps/billing/pdf.py:58) is a bare
# `weasyprint.HTML(string=html_string).write_pdf()` — no `xmp_metadata`, no
# `stylesheets` argument, no custom `url_fetcher` at all — so neither
# vulnerable code path is ever reached.
# Installed 69.0, fixed in 70.0. The `weasyprint>=62.0,<70` pin in
# apps/api/pyproject.toml predates any documented reason (present since the
# dependency's introduction, no changelog entry explaining the cap) —
# bumping past it isn't attempted here to avoid trading a non-exploitable
# finding for an unverified compatibility risk in PDF generation (customer
# invoices). Revisit the pin's reason before the next re-verification.
# expires: 2026-12-12
PYSEC-2026-3940
